<article class="offering-card">

            <div class="offering-photo" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/assets/images/winterizing.png"></div>

            <div class="offering-body">

              <h2 id="winterizing">RV Winterizing</h2>

              <p>Winterizing? We're in Texas... not Wisconsin... Correct, but think back to a few recent winters with
                temperatures remaining
                below freezing for several days. Harse winter days don't last all that long here. But they can easily do
                as much costly damage to your RV plumbing in those few days as they would in Wisconsin.
                If the weather forecast says drip your pipes at home, your RV needs attention.
                Winterizing consists of draining and flushing all of the water from all pipes, water heater, fresh water
                tank, and low point drains. After flushing, the pipes are
                filled with food-safe RV anti-freeze. De-winterizing reverses the process by flushing out the antifreeze
                and refilling with fresh water.

